  "worked_solution": "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=3$ for  $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=\u27e80,\\,0,\\,1\u27e9.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =\u27e84y,\\,5x,\\,2z\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80,\\,0,\\,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84y,\\,5x,\\,6\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80,\\,0,\\,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{S}6\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6{\u222c}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
